{
  "what": "Agreement of Ourea NISAR land/water maps with an independent Sentinel-1 water map",
  "nisar_maps": "handoff/layers/landwater/<date>.png (NISAR L2 GCOV, stand-in rule HH < -13 dB)",
  "nisar_codes_found": {
    "2026-07-11": [
      0,
      255
    ],
    "2026-08-28": [
      0,
      255
    ],
    "2026-10-03": [
      0,
      255
    ]
  },
  "method": {
    "reference_product": "Microsoft Planetary Computer sentinel-1-rtc (Sentinel-1 IW GRDH, RTC gamma0, 10 m)",
    "resampling": "10 m linear gamma0 averaged onto the 2048x2048 NISAR grid (EPSG:32648, ~32x30 m), then dB",
    "water_rule": "VV < -18.0 dB, then 3x3 majority (same clean-up as the NISAR rule); Otsu-threshold variant also reported",
    "excluded": "NISAR 128 (no data) and Sentinel-1 no-data pixels",
    "rice_200": "scored twice: as land and as water",
    "positive_class": "water; NISAR treated as prediction, Sentinel-1 as reference",
    "new_flood_water": "water on the pass date AND land on 2026-07-11 (each sensor vs its own baseline)",
    "land_cover_for_notes": "ESA WorldCover 2021 v200 (Planetary Computer esa-worldcover), mode-resampled",
    "script": "pipeline/validate_s1.py",
    "run_on": "2026-11-15"
  },
  "caveats": [
    "This is agreement between two radars, not accuracy against ground truth. Neither map is truth.",
    "The NISAR maps contain only codes 0 and 255 (no 128 no-data, no 200 rice yet), so both rice variants give identical numbers and nothing is excluded.",
    "NISAR maps use the stand-in rule HH < -13 dB, which is lenient; the fixed S1 rule VV < -18 dB is strict. Most disagreement is NISAR water / S1 land on cropland (paddies): smooth wet or bare soil and shallow water among young rice. Who is right there is unknown without field data.",
    "C-band (S1, 5.5 cm) sees less water under rice than L-band (NISAR, 24 cm), so S1 under-counts water under crops; the low precision is partly an S1 limitation, not only a NISAR error.",
    "Sentinel-1 is 1 day (main) or 2 days (check) after NISAR; water can rise or drain in between. The two S1 passes themselves agree only 92-98%, and their August water share differs (18% vs 11%).",
    "Permanent rivers/canals: S1 calls only ~45-49% of WorldCover 'permanent water' pixels water (wind-roughened river surface, mixed 30 m pixels on narrow canals); NISAR ~74%.",
    "The Otsu split on each S1 scene is reported too; on the July scene Otsu (-9.9 dB) is not a water/land split (the scene has almost no open water) and should be ignored.",
    "Sentinel-2 optical was not used: 76-95% cloud on these dates (handoff/layers/optical/optical.json)."
  ],
